<Base title={"Hotel Loans for Bankers Who Do Not Run Hotels"} description={"Seven things a community banker needs to understand before reading a hotel operating statement or answering a franchise notice: rates, the flag, the improvement plan, management, and more."}>
  <Hero eyebrow="For community banks" title={"Hotel loans for bankers who do not run hotels."} lead={"Seven things to understand before you read an operating statement or answer a franchise notice."} />
  <SectionNav section="communityBanks" />
  <main id="main">
<section class="section"><div class="wrap">
      <div class="head"><p class="eyebrow">A short primer</p><h2>A hotel is a business that reprices every night.</h2>
        <p>An office tenant signs a lease for years. A hotel sells tonight's rooms at tonight's price, with tonight's staff, under a brand it does not own. That is why a hotel loan behaves differently when it goes wrong.</p></div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><h3>The three numbers</h3><p><a href="/glossary/#occupancy">Occupancy</a> is the share of rooms sold. <a href="/glossary/#adr">Average daily rate</a> is what each sold room earned. <a href="/glossary/#revpar">RevPAR</a> combines the two. Each is read against the <a href="/glossary/#comp-set">comp set</a>, usually in an <a href="/glossary/#str-report">STR report</a>.</p></li>
        <li><h3>The flag</h3><p>The brand supplies the name, the reservation system, and the loyalty programme that bring much of a hotel's demand, in return for fees. A hotel that loses its flag usually loses much of its demand. The <a href="/glossary/#franchise-agreement">franchise agreement</a> is between the brand and the owner. The bank is not a party to it.</p></li>
        <li><h3>The comfort letter</h3><p>The bank's rights with the brand come from a separate <a href="/glossary/#comfort-letter">comfort letter</a>, usually notice of a franchise default, time to cure it, and a path to a new franchise if the bank takes title. Whether it passes to a buyer of the loan depends on its terms.</p></li>
        <li><h3>The improvement plan</h3><p>A <a href="/glossary/#pip">property improvement plan</a> is the brand's list of renovations, with deadlines. It is commonly triggered by a change of ownership, a renewal, or a failed inspection. An unfunded plan can put the flag at risk.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Who runs it</h3><p>Many community-bank hotel loans are owner-operated. Others are run by a management company under a <a href="/glossary/#management-agreement">management agreement</a>. Whether that agreement can be ended on a transfer is a key question, and a change of manager is often the first step in a recovery.</p></li>
        <li><h3>The rest of the business</h3><p>A liquor licence, a payroll, occupancy and sales taxes collected for the government, advance deposits, and an <a href="/glossary/#ffe-reserve">FF&amp;E reserve</a>. All of it has to carry over, or be dealt with, when the hotel changes hands.</p></li>
        <li><h3>Why speed matters</h3><p>A closed hotel loses its staff, its bookings, and often its flag. Most of the value depends on continuous operation, which is why a long foreclosure costs more on a hotel than on most property.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </div></section>

